"Search & Fetch | DeepSeek API Docs (https://api-docs.deepseek.com/guides/search)\nciteturn41342view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://api-docs.deepseek.com/guides/search\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://api-docs.deepseek.com/guides/search/; Total lines: 263\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGemini 4 Argon is now available for everyone (https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/introducing-gemini-4-argon/)\nciteturn41342view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn41340view2\",\"pattern\":\"Oct\"}); Total lines: 340\nL107: ### Innovation & AI\nL108: \nL109: See all in Innovation & AI\nL110: \nL111:     * Models & Research\nL112: \nL113:       * cite2†Google DeepMind L114:       * cite3†Google Research L115:       * cite4†Google Labs L116:       * cite5†Gemini models L117:       * cite6†Quantum computing L118:       * cite7†See all L119:     * Products\nL120: \nL121:       * cite8†Developer tools L122:       * cite9†Gemini app L123:       * cite10†Gemini Notebook L124:       * cite11†See all L125:     * Infrastructure & cloud\nL126: \nL127:       * cite12†Global network L128:       * cite13†Google Cloud L129:       * cite14†See all L130:     * Technology\nL131:       * cite15†Safety & Security L132:       * cite16†Health L133:       * cite17†See all L134: \nL135: Learn more:\nL136: \nL137: cite18†Google DeepMind blog†deepmind.google cite19†Google Research blog†research.google cite20†Google Developers blog†developers.googleblog.com cite21†Google Cloud blog†cloud.google.com L138: \nL139:   * Products & platforms\nL140: ### Products & platforms\nL141: \nL142: See all in Products & platforms\nL143: \nL144:     * Products\nL145: \nL146:       * cite22†Search L147:       * cite23†Maps L148:       * cite24†Chrome L149:       * cite25†Google Health L150:       * cite26†Google Workspace L151:       * cite27†Learning & Education L152:       * cite28†Shopping L153:       * cite29†See all L154:     * Platforms\nL155: \nL156:       * cite30†Android L157:       * cite31†Google Play L158:       * cite32†Wear OS L159:       * cite33†See all L160:     * Devices\nL161:       * cite34†Pixel L162:       * cite35†Google Nest L163:       * cite36†Fitbit L164:       * cite37†Chromebook L165:       * cite38†Googlebook L166:       * cite39†See all L167: \nL168: Learn more:\nL169: \nL170: cite40†Google Ads & Commerce blog cite41†Waze blog L171: \nL172:   * Company news\nL173: ### Company news\nL174: \nL175: See all in Company news\nL176: \nL177:     * Outreach & initiatives\nL178: \nL179:       * cite42†Creating opportunity L180:       * cite15†Safety & security L181:       * cite43†Google.org L182:       * cite44†Public policy L183:       * cite45†Sustainability L184:       * cite16†Health L185:       * cite46†See all L186:     * Leadership\nL187: \nL188:       * cite47†Sundar Pichai, CEO L189:       * cite48†More authors L190:       * cite48†See all L191:     * Inside Google\nL192: \nL193:       * cite49†Around the globe L194:       * cite50†Life at Google L195:       * cite51†See all L196: \nL197: Learn more:\nL198: \nL199: cite52†Google Security blog L200: \nL201:   * cite1†Feed L202: Gemini 4 Argon: Built to solve your hardest problems\nL203: \nL204: []\nL205: \nL206: Preferences\nL207: \nL208:   * [Select]\nL209: \nL210: Links\nL211: \nL212:   * cite53†Images L213:   * cite54†RSS feed L214: # Gemini 4 Argon: Built to solve your hardest problems\nL215: \nL216: Oct 13, 2026\nL217: \nL218: |\nL219: \nL220: We’re giving developers, enterprises, scientists, and consumers access to our latest frontier model.\nL221: \nL222: * * *\nL223: \nL224: cite55†Koray Kavukcuoglu SVP, Google DeepMind and Chief AI Architect, Google L225: \nL226: * * *\nL227: \nL228: cite56†TBD†storage.googleapis.com L229: \nL230: In this article\nL231: \nL232: * * *\nL233:   * cite57†Built to solve your hardest problems L234:   * cite58†Solving complex engineering tasks L235:   * cite59†Transforming critical business and professional workflows L236:   * cite60†Accelerating real-world impact with researchers and domain experts L237:   * cite61†Developing Argon responsibly and safely L238:   * cite62†Get started today L239: cite63†Gemini 4 Argon is now globally available today for developers and enterprises via the API in cite64†AI Studio†ai.google.dev and cite65†Gemini platform†console.cloud.google.com , for cite66†Google AI Ultra subscribers†one.google.com in cite67†Google Antigravity†antigravity.google , and Google AI Ultra and Pro subscribers in the cite68†Gemini app†gemini.google and cite69†AI Mode in Search†aimode.google .\nL240: Argon excels at deep work in complex, real-world tasks across domains including coding, finance, legal, cybersecurity, and science. Its depth and precision across multi-step tasks leads Argon to outperform other frontier models on cite70†benchmarks across domains , ranking #1 on the cite71†Vals Index†www.vals.ai , which measures model performance across critical economic sectors like finance, coding, legal, and tax work.\nL241: The model launches today at an cite72†introductory price†aistudio.google.com cite73†^{1} of $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens (with cached input tokens at 95% off).\nL242: \nL243: Teams at Google, across enterprises, and in the research community have already been using Argon to land significant impact across fields:\nL244:   * To solve major engineering problems like large codebase refactoring and optimizations\nL245:   * To optimize business workflows across domains like legal, finance, and science\nL246:   * To find and patch critical cybersecurity vulnerabilities\nL247:   * To enable novel breakthroughs in quantum computing, nuclear fusion, and life sciences\nL248: ## Solving complex engineering tasks\nL249: \nL250: At Google, we’ve been using Argon on our hardest engineering problems. Over the past few weeks, software engineers have been shipping high-quality code faster than ever. Argon in Antigravity has helped teams increase agentic code submissions by more than 35% while reducing code rollbacks by nearly a third.\nL251:   * Untangling browser feature code in Chrome: Argon helped the Chrome team separate core webpage handling from a ~6 million line legacy folder of browser features held within Chromium’s 40 million-line codebase. cite74†Piping multiple clean code changes at a time†issues.chromium.org , Argon helped the team finish an architectural refactor across hundreds of files, completing months of work in weeks.\nL252: For Chrome's billions of global users, this will unlock the ability for live webpages to move fluidly between tabs, side panels, and floating windows without reloading, eliminating an entire category of browser crashes.\nL253:   * Optimizing a production Python codebase in hours: Argon in Antigravity is helping the team behind Google's open-source cite75†Agent Development Kit†github.com (ADK), a framework powering enterprise agents, push their performance engineering further. The team gave Argon their cite76†.agents/skills†github.com rulebook and a one-paragraph goal to eliminate framework overhead without breaking existing apps.\nL254: Working autonomously overnight, Argon surfaced optimizations, cite77†now live in google/adk-python†github.com , that cut in-memory session prep time by 40% in long sessions and sped up cold imports by 21%. Read the cite78†engineering deep dive†developers.googleblog.com to learn more.\nL255:   * Quantum chemistry open-source package optimization: xTB (Semiempirical Extended Tight-Binding) is one of the most-cited open-source quantum chemistry packages for molecules. Scientists use it to predict molecules' 3D shapes and to screen large numbers of drug and materials candidates. Using Argon in Antigravity, our researchers made xTB up to 2.3x faster at predicting molecular structures and 1.8x faster at conformer search.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn-Device AI Against Scams: Google DeepMind & MDDI — Google DeepMind (https://deepmind.google/blog/on-device-ai-scam-detection-mddi/)\nciteturn41342view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn41340view3\",\"pattern\":\"October\"}); Total lines: 206\nL61: ## Our latest AI breakthroughs and updates from the lab\nL62: \nL63: cite10†Explore research L64: \nL65: Breakthroughs\nL66: \nL67: cite11†SIMA 2 An agent that plays, reasons, and learns with you cite7†Genie 3 Generate and explore interactive worlds cite12†AlphaGo Mastering the game of Go cite8†Gemini Robotics Perceive, reason, use tools and interact L68: \nL69: Learn more\nL70: \nL71: cite13†Evals cite14†Publications cite15†Responsibility cite16†Frontier safety L72: \nL73: Science\nL74: ## Unlocking a new era of discovery with AI\nL75: \nL76: cite17†Explore science L77: \nL78: Breakthroughs\nL79: \nL80: cite18†AlphaFold Predict protein structures with high accuracy cite19†AlphaGenome Using AI to understand the human genome cite20†WeatherNext Fast and accurate AI weather forecasting cite21†AlphaEarth Map our planet in unprecedented detail cite22†AlphaEvolve Design advanced algorithms for math and applications in computing L81: \nL82: Learn more\nL83: cite23†Gemini for Science†ai.google cite24†Experimental Tools†labs.google cite25†Science on Antigravity†antigravity.google L84: \nL85: About\nL86: ## Our mission is to build AI responsibly to benefit humanity\nL87: \nL88: cite26†About Google DeepMind L89: \nL90: Learn more\nL91: \nL92: cite29†Education cite30†Our National Partnerships for AI cite31†Accelerator programs cite32†The Podcast cite33†DeepMind Institute†institute.deepmind.com L93: \nL94: cite15†Responsibility Ensuring AI safety through proactive security, even against evolving threats cite27†News Discover our latest AI breakthroughs, projects, and updates cite28†Careers We’re looking for people who want to make a real, positive impact on the world L95: cite34†Build with Gemini†aistudio.google.com cite35†Try Gemini†gemini.google.com L96: \nL97: cite36†Google DeepMind L98: \nL99: cite37†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite38†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI cite39†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google cite40†Google Research Explore our research†research.google L100: \nL101: Products and apps\nL102: cite35†Gemini app Chat with Gemini†gemini.google.com cite34†Google AI Studio Build with our next-gen models†aistudio.google.com cite41†Google Antigravity Our agentic development platform†antigravity.google L103: \nL104: cite0†Models L105: \nL106: cite10†Research L107: \nL108: cite17†Science L109: \nL110: cite26†About L111: \nL112: cite34†Build with Gemini†aistudio.google.com cite35†Try Gemini†gemini.google.com L113: \nL114: October 14, 2026 Responsibility & Safety\nL115: # Google DeepMind and Singapore Government collaborate to build fine-tuned AI model for scam detection\nL116: \nL117: JJ Geewax and Miguel de Andrés\nL118: \nL119: cite42†Image: Abstract 3D design featuring wavy layers of glossy red and textured white akin to the Singapore flag, adorned with glass spheres and a pixelated red block formation†lh3.googleusercontent.com L120: \nL121: Collaborating with the Government Technology Agency of Singapore (GovTech Singapore) to protect users without compromising privacy.\nL122: Scams, such as investment fraud, fake job offers, and impersonation of public officials make up over half of Singapore’s reported crimes, and account for more than cite43†S$4 billion in losses since 2019†www.straitstimes.com . At the same time, scam tactics have shifted from simple phishing to complex social engineering, whereby attackers manipulate victims with convincing conversations before requesting money. Such scams now account for up to 80% of all losses.\nL123: To address this, Google DeepMind and the Government Technology Agency of Singapore (GovTech Singapore) have been working together as part of our cite44†National AI Partnership with the Singapore Government. Together, we've fine-tuned a privacy-preserving, on-device scam detection model.\nL124: ## On-device AI protection without compromising privacy\nL125: Generic models often struggle with local deception techniques, making scam detection harder. To address this, Google’s lightweight Gemma model was fine-tuned on local scam conversations. These ranged from impersonations of public institutions, banks, and local figures, to trending regional schemes. This ensures the fine-tuned model will be able to distinguish benign social chatter from genuine manipulation when deployed in applications installed by citizens.\nL126: Unlike traditional threat detection, which relies on server-side scanning of messages in the cloud, this model can process conversation data directly on-device. When integrated into applications, this will allow faster analysis of conversation patterns to detect manipulative intent and help citizens act before financial or credential exposure can occur.\nL127: The roll out of the model within applications will be carefully phased and developed in partnership with the Singapore Government. When deployed, the model will only be able to access on-device conversations with the user's consent, in accordance with the terms of use of that application. As the model runs entirely on-device, neither the Singapore Government nor Google, will be able to access users’ conversations through its deployment.\nL128: > Fighting scams shouldn't mean sacrificing personal privacy. Working with Google DeepMind, we're bringing advanced AI protection directly onto citizens' devices. This allows us to catch highly targeted, local scam attempts in real time while keeping personal communications private.\nL129: \nL130: Mark Chen\nL131: \nL132: Director, Trust and Safety, GovTech Singapore\nL133: ## Under the hood: local context and edge efficiency\nL134: \nL135: When the model detects manipulative conversational patterns, it triggers a real-time warning directly on the user's device advising them to pause and exercise caution before sharing sensitive information or funds.\nL136: \nL137: Running a complex scam classifier model on modern smartphones is no easy task, with battery efficiency being a critical area of concern. To do this, we're relying on a two-stage pipeline:\nL138: cite45†Image: A flowchart illustrating a two-stage message screening system. Stage 1 uses an on-device \"Lightweight Fast Screening Classifier\" to filter incoming messages; safe messages receive a \"Benign Verdict\", while suspicious ones proceed to Stage 2. Stage 2 uses a \"Fine-tuned Gemma Model\" for multi-turn conversation analysis, resulting in either a \"Scam Alert\" or a \"Benign Verdict.\"†storage.googleapis.com 【46†Image: A flowchart illustrating a two-stage message screening system.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeveloping Gemini 4 Argon responsibly and safely (https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/safety-security/gemini-4-model-safety-security/?utm_campaign=gdm&utm_content=&utm_medium=referral&utm_source=deepmind.google)\nciteturn41342view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn41340view4\",\"pattern\":\"Oct\"}); Total lines: 288\nL107: ### Innovation & AI\nL108: \nL109: See all in Innovation & AI\nL110: \nL111:     * Models & Research\nL112: \nL113:       * cite2†Google DeepMind L114:       * cite3†Google Research L115:       * cite4†Google Labs L116:       * cite5†Gemini models L117:       * cite6†Quantum computing L118:       * cite7†See all L119:     * Products\nL120: \nL121:       * cite8†Developer tools L122:       * cite9†Gemini app L123:       * cite10†Gemini Notebook L124:       * cite11†See all L125:     * Infrastructure & cloud\nL126: \nL127:       * cite12†Global network L128:       * cite13†Google Cloud L129:       * cite14†See all L130:     * Technology\nL131:       * cite15†Safety & Security L132:       * cite16†Health L133:       * cite17†See all L134: \nL135: Learn more:\nL136: \nL137: cite18†Google DeepMind blog†deepmind.google cite19†Google Research blog†research.google cite20†Google Developers blog†developers.googleblog.com cite21†Google Cloud blog†cloud.google.com L138: \nL139:   * Products & platforms\nL140: ### Products & platforms\nL141: \nL142: See all in Products & platforms\nL143: \nL144:     * Products\nL145: \nL146:       * cite22†Search L147:       * cite23†Maps L148:       * cite24†Chrome L149:       * cite25†Google Health L150:       * cite26†Google Workspace L151:       * cite27†Learning & Education L152:       * cite28†Shopping L153:       * cite29†See all L154:     * Platforms\nL155: \nL156:       * cite30†Android L157:       * cite31†Google Play L158:       * cite32†Wear OS L159:       * cite33†See all L160:     * Devices\nL161:       * cite34†Pixel L162:       * cite35†Google Nest L163:       * cite36†Fitbit L164:       * cite37†Chromebook L165:       * cite38†Googlebook L166:       * cite39†See all L167: \nL168: Learn more:\nL169: \nL170: cite40†Google Ads & Commerce blog cite41†Waze blog L171: \nL172:   * Company news\nL173: ### Company news\nL174: \nL175: See all in Company news\nL176: \nL177:     * Outreach & initiatives\nL178: \nL179:       * cite42†Creating opportunity L180:       * cite15†Safety & security L181:       * cite43†Google.org L182:       * cite44†Public policy L183:       * cite45†Sustainability L184:       * cite16†Health L185:       * cite46†See all L186:     * Leadership\nL187: \nL188:       * cite47†Sundar Pichai, CEO L189:       * cite48†More authors L190:       * cite48†See all L191:     * Inside Google\nL192: \nL193:       * cite49†Around the globe L194:       * cite50†Life at Google L195:       * cite51†See all L196: \nL197: Learn more:\nL198: \nL199: cite52†Google Security blog L200: \nL201:   * cite1†Feed L202: Developing Gemini 4 Argon responsibly and safely\nL203: \nL204: []\nL205: \nL206: Preferences\nL207: \nL208:   * [Select]\nL209: \nL210: Links\nL211: \nL212:   * cite53†Images L213:   * cite54†RSS feed L214: # Developing Gemini 4 Argon responsibly and safely\nL215: \nL216: Oct 13, 2026\nL217: \nL218: |\nL219: \nL220: Learn more about our approach to safety and security for Gemini 4 Argon.\nL221: \nL222: * * *\nL223: \nL224: Anca Dragan\nL225: \nL226: VP, AI Safety & Behavior\nL227: \nL228: Raluca Ada Popa\nL229: \nL230: Head of Gemini Security, Google DeepMind\nL231: \nL232: * * *\nL233: \nL234: cite55†Image: Gemini 4 Argon logo next to a large white \"4\", all on a gradient blue background†storage.googleapis.com L235: Gemini 4 Argon is a step forward in what models can do — especially when it comes to deep work in complex, real-world tasks across domains including coding, finance, legal, cybersecurity, and science. Its cyberdefense capabilities are frontier-level and designed to give defenders an advantage over attackers.\nL236: To maximize its potential, we first wanted cyber defenders to put it to work via our cite56†Fairwind Program†deepmind.google , while our own security teams used it to scan critical systems inside and outside of Google.\nL237: This window of limited access showed why these capabilities matter. For instance, cite57†Wiz†www.wiz.io used Argon to uncover a critical bug in hospital software that previous models had missed, helping protect hundreds of thousands of patient records. Meanwhile, Google’s own security teams used Argon to find and patch bugs across internal software or open-source software.\nL238: This included fixing foundational vulnerabilities affecting systems globally, which are going through our responsible disclosure process.\nL239: Today, we are beginning to roll out cite58†Argon broadly , as well as expanding our Fairwind Program to additional trusted cyber defenders. To do this well, we grappled with two big questions:\nL240:   1. How do we build the right safety guardrails? If Argon can help defenders find complex software bugs or help scientists design experiments, how do we stop bad actors from using it to launch cyberattacks or create biological threats? (Misuse)\nL241:   2. Can we trust the model to do the job the right way? As models become more resourceful problem-solvers, how do we make sure they don’t cut corners, cross boundaries, or make incorrect high-stakes decisions? (Misalignment and Prompt Injection)\nL242: Our cite59†full Safety and Security Report†storage.googleapis.com systematically covers our set of defenses. Below are some highlights from how we approached these two questions for Gemini 4 Argon.\nL243: ## Misuse prevention across high-risk domains\nL244: The same coding and reasoning improvements that enabled Argon to find and patch vulnerabilities also drove better performance on our cybersecurity tests — solving all 12 of the end-to-end simulated cyberattack scenarios, up from five in our previous generation. In biology evaluations, unguarded versions of the model also showed a much stronger ability to help troubleshoot complex lab procedures step by step.\nL245: Following our cite60†Frontier Safety Framework†deepmind.google , since we saw elevated capabilities in the cyber and biological domains, we put our next-generation set of frontier safeguards in place across both areas.\nL246: First, when it comes to potentially dangerous requests — from writing exploit code to helping with dangerous biological materials, Argon is significantly better at not fulfilling harmful requests than our previous models. On an adversarial set meant to stress test the model, violations reduced nearly twofold — also significantly better than previous Gemini models. Second, we wrap the model in three layers of real-time protection; this is to prevent determined attackers that might try to jailbreak (i.e.\nL247: trick) the model by disguising a dangerous request as a harmless story or breaking it into dozens of small questions over several weeks. Here is how these protections work:\nL248:   * Input screening: Smaller models check incoming requests and conversation context for malicious intent or dangerous requests.\nL249:   * Internal introspection: Even if a request is cleverly disguised to look harmless, small transformers (\"activation probes\") read the model’s internal neural signals and are trained to spot when it is activating knowledge about cyberattacks or biological threats.\nL250:   * Continuous monitoring: As the model reasons and writes its response across multiple steps, we combine these signals and take action when necessary such as cutting off the agent mid-stream if the responses become problematic.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGemini 4 Argon for Robotics - Google Developers Blog (https://developers.googleblog.com/gemini-4-argon-for-robotics?utm_campaign=gdm&utm_content=&utm_medium=referral&utm_source=deepmind.google)\nciteturn41342view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn41340view5\",\"pattern\":\"October\"}); Redirected to URL: https://developers.googleblog.com/gemini-4-argon-for-robotics/?utm_campaign=gdm&utm_content=&utm_medium=referral&utm_source=deepmind.google; Total lines: 333\nNo matching text found for \"October\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmbeddingGemma 2 is a best-in-class open model for natively multimodal embeddings (https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/developers-tools/embeddinggemma-2/?utm_campaign=gdm&utm_content=&utm_medium=referral&utm_source=deepmind.google)\nciteturn41342view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn41340view6\",\"pattern\":\"Oct\"}); Total lines: 299\nL107: ### Innovation & AI\nL108: \nL109: See all in Innovation & AI\nL110: \nL111:     * Models & Research\nL112: \nL113:       * cite2†Google DeepMind L114:       * cite3†Google Research L115:       * cite4†Google Labs L116:       * cite5†Gemini models L117:       * cite6†Quantum computing L118:       * cite7†See all L119:     * Products\nL120: \nL121:       * cite8†Developer tools L122:       * cite9†Gemini app L123:       * cite10†Gemini Notebook L124:       * cite11†See all L125:     * Infrastructure & cloud\nL126: \nL127:       * cite12†Global network L128:       * cite13†Google Cloud L131:       * cite15†Safety & Security L132:       * cite16†Health L133:       * cite17†See all L134: \nL135: Learn more:\nL136: \nL137: cite18†Google DeepMind blog†deepmind.google cite19†Google Research blog†research.google cite20†Google Developers blog†developers.googleblog.com cite21†Google Cloud blog†cloud.google.com L138: \nL139:   * Products & platforms\nL140: ### Products & platforms\nL141: \nL142: See all in Products & platforms\nL143: \nL144:     * Products\nL145: \nL146:       * cite22†Search L147:       * cite23†Maps L148:       * cite24†Chrome L149:       * cite25†Google Health L150:       * cite26†Google Workspace L151:       * cite27†Learning & Education L152:       * cite28†Shopping L153:       * cite29†See all L154:     * Platforms\nL155: \nL156:       * cite30†Android L157:       * cite31†Google Play L158:       * cite32†Wear OS L159:       * cite33†See all L160:     * Devices\nL161:       * cite34†Pixel L162:       * cite35†Google Nest L163:       * cite36†Fitbit L164:       * cite37†Chromebook L165:       * cite38†Googlebook L166:       * cite39†See all L167: \nL168: Learn more:\nL169: \nL170: cite40†Google Ads & Commerce blog cite41†Waze blog L171: \nL172:   * Company news\nL173: ### Company news\nL174: \nL175: See all in Company news\nL176: \nL177:     * Outreach & initiatives\nL178: \nL179:       * cite42†Creating opportunity L180:       * cite15†Safety & security L181:       * cite43†Google.org L182:       * cite44†Public policy L183:       * cite45†Sustainability L184:       * cite16†Health L185:       * cite46†See all L186:     * Leadership\nL187: \nL188:       * cite47†Sundar Pichai, CEO L189:       * cite48†More authors L190:       * cite48†See all L191:     * Inside Google\nL192: \nL193:       * cite49†Around the globe L194:       * cite50†Life at Google L195:       * cite51†See all L196: \nL197: Learn more:\nL198: \nL199: cite52†Google Security blog L200: \nL201:   * cite1†Feed L202: EmbeddingGemma 2: an open, lightweight multimodal embedding model\nL203: \nL204: []\nL205: \nL206: Preferences\nL207: \nL208:   * [Select]\nL209: \nL210: Links\nL211: \nL212:   * cite53†Images L213:   * cite54†RSS feed L214: # EmbeddingGemma 2: an open, lightweight multimodal embedding model\nL215: \nL216: Oct 06, 2026\nL217: \nL218: |\nL219: \nL220: EmbeddingGemma 2 is the most capable model for on-device multimodal embeddings, natively mapping combinations of text, images, audio, and video into a unified embedding space.\nL221: \nL222: * * *\nL223: \nL224: Sahil Dua\nL225: \nL226: Research Engineer, Google DeepMind\nL227: \nL228: Henrique Schechter Vera\nL229: \nL230: Research Engineer, Google DeepMind\nL231: \nL232: * * *\nL233: \nL234: cite55†Image: EmbeddingGemma 2†storage.googleapis.com L235: \nL236: Your browser does not support the audio element.\nL237: \nL238: Listen to article\nL239: [[duration]] minutes\nL240: \nL241: This content is generated by Google AI. Generative AI is experimental\nL242: \nL243: [Input]\nL244: \nL245: Voice Speed\nL246: \nL247: Voice\nL248: \nL249: Speed 0.75X 1X 1.5X 2X\nL250: We introduced cite56†EmbeddingGemma†developers.googleblog.com last year to provide a lightweight option for high-quality text embeddings, to help your apps organize, search, and connect information directly on consumer hardware. The developer community’s response blew past our expectations. With more than 20 million downloads, builders have used it to power smarter on-device search tools and privacy-first retrieval augmented generation (RAG) pipelines.\nL251: Today, we’re launching EmbeddingGemma 2, expanding beyond text to unify code, images, video, and audio in a shared embedding space. Built on the Gemma 4 architecture and released under a commercially permissive Apache 2.0 license, EmbeddingGemma 2 has 740 million parameters, making it optimal for on-device inference. It can help find a specific video clip from a voice memo, or search through hours of audio recordings based on a text query, all processed by a single, natively multimodal model.\nL252: Built from the same technology as Gemini Embedding models, EmbeddingGemma 2 is:\nL253:   * Best-in-class for its size: Achieves leading scores among sub-1B multimodal embedders for its size across benchmarks like MTEB (Massive Text Embedding Benchmark) Code and MAEB (Massive Audio Embedding Benchmark), while matching or outperforming many larger models across text, vision, and audio tasks.\nL254:   * Modular by design: Requires as little as 270M parameters for text-only workloads with optional vision (170M) and audio (300M) encoders for full multimodal support.\nL255:   * Storage-efficient: Using Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL), developers can dynamically truncate output vectors from 768 dimensions down to 512, 256, or 128 dimensions. This provides up to 6x storage reduction for local vector databases and memory usage.\nL256:   * Optimized for on-device performance: Runs efficiently within tight resource constraints. With quantization, on a Google Pixel 11 Pro, EmbeddingGemma 2 requires as little as ~191MB active RAM for text-only weights and ~567MB for the full multimodal model.\nL257:   * Extended context ready: Features an 8K token context window (4x larger than EmbeddingGemma 1), allowing it to process up to 5.5 minutes of audio, 29 images, 58 video frames, or interleaved combinations thereof directly on local hardware.\nL258: ## Achieving top-tier quality for code, vision, and audio\nL259: EmbeddingGemma 2 matches the strong multilingual text performance of EmbeddingGemma while delivering a significant 9.92-point improvement on code performance (in MTEB Code, from 68.76 to 78.68), making it well-suited for local codebase indexing, semantic code search, and coding agent retrieval. Across image, video, documents, and audio, it sets a new standard in quality-per-parameter for sub-1B models and even outperforms some specialist models more than twice its size.\nL260: cite57†Image: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark (Code)†storage.googleapis.com L261: \nL262: cite58†Image: Massive Image Embedding Benchmark (Lite)†storage.googleapis.com L263: \nL264: cite59†Image: Massive Audio Embedding Benchmark†storage.googleapis.com L265: \nL266: Find full evaluation metrics and model information in the cite60†EmbeddingGemma 2 model card†ai.google.dev .\nL267: ## Enabling semantic search, routing, and retrieval, fully on-device\nL268: \nL269: EmbeddingGemma 2 brings robust capabilities directly to edge hardware. Generating embeddings locally helps ensure data privacy, reduces pipeline latency, and empowers developers to build cross-modal search and retrieval that works entirely offline.\n"
